  <h2 id="duration"><span class="num">3</span>{{ t('The price of duration', '久期的价格') }}</h2>
  <div class="pod-copy l-en">
    <p>To understand why the current market feels internally contradictory, it helps to stop thinking about interest rates as something the Federal Reserve simply "sets."</p>
    <p>The Fed controls a very short-term policy rate.</p>
    <p>The market determines the price of lending money for ten, twenty or thirty years.</p>
    <p>Those are related prices, but they are not the same price.</p>
    <p>A 30-year Treasury investor is making a very different commitment from an investor holding overnight cash.</p>
    <p>That investor is accepting decades of inflation risk, fiscal risk, opportunity cost and uncertainty. In exchange, the investor demands compensation.</p>
    <p>When the supply of long-term bonds rises, or when investors become less willing to hold them at the previous yield, that compensation has to increase.</p>
    <p>The easiest way to understand the present regime is to imagine a single giant auction for long-term capital.</p>
    <p>On one side are all the borrowers.</p>
    <p>The U.S. Treasury is there.</p>
    <p>The hyperscalers are there.</p>
    <p>Utilities are there.</p>
    <p>Data-center developers are there.</p>
    <p>Companies building power generation are there.</p>
    <p>Defense contractors, industrial companies, mortgage borrowers and ordinary corporations are there too.</p>
    <p>On the other side are the savers.</p>
    <p>Pension funds.</p>
    <p>Insurance companies.</p>
    <p>Banks.</p>
    <p>Money-market funds.</p>
    <p>Foreign reserve managers.</p>
    <p>Sovereign investors.</p>
    <p>Households.</p>
    <p>Asset managers.</p>
    <p>Japanese institutions.</p>
    <p>European institutions.</p>
    <p>Every other pool of capital willing to hold a claim that matures years from now.</p>
    <p>The yield is the price that brings those two sides together.</p>
    <p>When the borrowers want dramatically more money, the yield rises until someone is willing to provide it.</p>
    <p>That is not a malfunction.</p>
    <p>That is the market working.</p>
    <p>The problem begins when the yield required to clear the market becomes high enough to damage the very economy and financial system that created the borrowing demand.</p>
    <p>This is where the current U.S. problem becomes unusual.</p>
    <p>The federal government has enormous financing needs.</p>
    <p>At the same time, the private sector has launched an infrastructure program of extraordinary scale.</p>
    <p>Artificial intelligence is often described as software because the user interacts with models through software.</p>
    <p>Economically, however, the current AI boom is one of the most physical technology cycles in modern history.</p>
    <p>Training and running models requires accelerators.</p>
    <p>Accelerators require memory.</p>
    <p>The systems require networking.</p>
    <p>The racks require power distribution.</p>
    <p>The buildings require transformers.</p>
    <p>The chips require cooling.</p>
    <p>The data centers require land, substations, transmission, generators and increasingly dedicated power supply.</p>
    <p>All of that requires capital before it produces output.</p>
    <p>The early phase of the AI boom could be financed largely through the exceptional cash generation of the largest technology companies.</p>
    <p>That is changing.</p>
    <p>As spending grows, corporate borrowing grows with it.</p>
    <p>Project-finance structures grow.</p>
    <p>Utilities need more capital.</p>
    <p>Data-center developers need more capital.</p>
    <p>Infrastructure funds need more capital.</p>
    <p>Even companies with enormous cash balances are increasingly using debt markets because the scale of the buildout is too large to treat financing as an afterthought.</p>
    <p>That is why AI is no longer just <strong>affected by</strong> the interest rate.</p>
    <p>AI is beginning to participate in <strong>setting</strong> the interest rate.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pod-copy l-zh">
    <p>要理解当前市场为何显得内在矛盾，首先要停止把利率理解成美联储简单“设定”的东西。</p>
    <p>美联储控制的是极短期政策利率。</p>
    <p>而借出十年、二十年或三十年资金的价格，由市场决定。</p>
    <p>这两类价格彼此相关，却不是同一个价格。</p>
    <p>30 年期美债投资者所作的承诺，与持有隔夜现金的投资者截然不同。</p>
    <p>这位投资者接受了几十年的通胀风险、财政风险、机会成本与不确定性，因此要求补偿。</p>
    <p>当长期债券供给增加，或投资者不再愿意按先前收益率持有它们时，这份补偿就必须上升。</p>
    <p>理解当前体制最简单的方法，是想象一场面向长期资本的巨型拍卖。</p>
    <p>一边是所有借款人。</p>
    <p>美国财政部在那里。</p>
    <p>超大规模云厂商在那里。</p>
    <p>公用事业公司在那里。</p>
    <p>数据中心开发商在那里。</p>
    <p>建设发电设施的公司在那里。</p>
    <p>国防承包商、工业公司、按揭借款人和普通企业也在那里。</p>
    <p>另一边是储蓄者。</p>
    <p>养老基金。</p>
    <p>保险公司。</p>
    <p>银行。</p>
    <p>货币市场基金。</p>
    <p>外国储备管理者。</p>
    <p>主权投资者。</p>
    <p>家庭。</p>
    <p>资产管理人。</p>
    <p>日本机构。</p>
    <p>欧洲机构。</p>
    <p>以及所有愿意持有多年后才到期债权的其他资本池。</p>
    <p>收益率，就是让两边相遇的价格。</p>
    <p>当借款人突然需要多得多的资金时，收益率就会上升，直到有人愿意提供资金。</p>
    <p>这不是故障。</p>
    <p>这正是市场在工作。</p>
    <p>问题始于：让市场出清所需的收益率，高到开始伤害创造借款需求的经济与金融体系本身。</p>
    <p>这正是当前美国问题的特殊之处。</p>
    <p>联邦政府有庞大的融资需求。</p>
    <p>与此同时，私营部门启动了一项规模异常巨大的基础设施计划。</p>
    <p>人工智能常被描述为软件，因为用户通过软件与模型交互。</p>
    <p>但从经济意义上说，当前 AI 热潮是现代史上最具物理性的科技周期之一。</p>
    <p>训练和运行模型需要加速器。</p>
    <p>加速器需要存储。</p>
    <p>系统需要网络。</p>
    <p>机架需要配电。</p>
    <p>建筑需要变压器。</p>
    <p>芯片需要散热。</p>
    <p>数据中心需要土地、变电站、输电、发电机，以及越来越多的专属电力供应。</p>
    <p>这一切都要先投入资本，之后才会产生产出。</p>
    <p>AI 热潮早期，主要可以依靠最大型科技公司异常强劲的现金创造能力融资。</p>
    <p>这正在改变。</p>
    <p>随着支出增长，企业借款也同步增长。</p>
    <p>项目融资结构不断扩大。</p>
    <p>公用事业需要更多资本。</p>
    <p>数据中心开发商需要更多资本。</p>
    <p>基础设施基金需要更多资本。</p>
    <p>即便坐拥巨额现金的公司也越来越多地使用债券市场，因为建设规模太大，融资不可能再被当作事后问题。</p>
    <p>因此，AI 已不再只是<strong>受利率影响</strong>。</p>
    <p>AI 正开始参与<strong>设定利率</strong>。</p>
  </div>

