  <div class="pod-copy l-en">
    <p>The feedback loop works like this.</p>
    <p>AI demand rises.</p>
    <p>Companies spend more.</p>
    <p>Capital expenditure rises.</p>
    <p>Debt issuance rises.</p>
    <p>Utilities and project vehicles borrow more.</p>
    <p>The supply of corporate duration rises alongside the supply of government duration.</p>
    <p>Investors demand more yield to absorb it all.</p>
    <p>Long-term yields rise.</p>
    <p>Higher yields increase the required return on new AI projects.</p>
    <p>At the margin, some projects that looked attractive at a lower cost of capital no longer clear their hurdle rate.</p>
    <p>Capital discipline improves.</p>
    <p>Weak projects are delayed or canceled.</p>
    <p>Stronger operators gain share.</p>
    <p>The AI boom moves from a scarcity contest to a capital-allocation contest.</p>
    <p>This is an important transition because it changes the kind of company the market should reward.</p>
    <p>During the first stage, scarcity was enough.</p>
    <p>If there were not enough GPUs, the GPU supplier won.</p>
    <p>If there was not enough HBM, the memory supplier won.</p>
    <p>If there were not enough optical modules, the optical supplier won.</p>
    <p>The investor did not have to know the long-run return on the entire AI system. The bottleneck itself created the economics.</p>
    <p>In the next stage, that becomes less sufficient.</p>
    <p>The market begins asking:</p>
    <p><strong>How much incremental revenue did this AI investment produce?</strong></p>
    <p><strong>How much cash did it consume?</strong></p>
    <p><strong>How much debt was required?</strong></p>
    <p><strong>How much dilution was required?</strong></p>
    <p><strong>How long will the hardware remain economically useful?</strong></p>
    <p><strong>How much depreciation is real rather than merely accounting?</strong></p>
    <p><strong>What is utilization?</strong></p>
    <p><strong>What is the residual value of the infrastructure if models or chips change faster than expected?</strong></p>
    <p>Those questions determine the capital efficiency of the AI buildout.</p>
    <p>And capital efficiency is what matters when capital itself becomes scarce.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pod-copy l-zh">
    <p>反馈回路如下运行。</p>
    <p>AI 需求上升。</p>
    <p>企业增加支出。</p>
    <p>资本开支上升。</p>
    <p>债券发行上升。</p>
    <p>公用事业与项目载体借入更多资金。</p>
    <p>企业久期供给与政府久期供给同时上升。</p>
    <p>投资者要求更高收益率，才愿意吸收这一切。</p>
    <p>长期收益率上升。</p>
    <p>更高收益率提高了新 AI 项目的最低必要回报。</p>
    <p>在边际上，一些在较低资本成本下看似有吸引力的项目，不再能够跨过门槛收益率。</p>
    <p>资本纪律改善。</p>
    <p>薄弱项目被推迟或取消。</p>
    <p>更强的运营商获得份额。</p>
    <p>AI 热潮从稀缺性竞赛转向资本配置竞赛。</p>
    <p>这是一次重要转折，因为它改变了市场应该奖励哪一类公司。</p>
    <p>第一阶段，稀缺就够了。</p>
    <p>GPU 不够，GPU 供应商就赢。</p>
    <p>HBM 不够，存储供应商就赢。</p>
    <p>光模块不够，光模块供应商就赢。</p>
    <p>投资者不必知道整套 AI 系统的长期回报。瓶颈本身就创造了经济性。</p>
    <p>下一阶段，仅凭稀缺越来越不够。</p>
    <p>市场开始追问：</p>
    <p><strong>这笔 AI 投资产生了多少增量收入？</strong></p>
    <p><strong>它消耗了多少现金？</strong></p>
    <p><strong>需要多少债务？</strong></p>
    <p><strong>需要多少股权稀释？</strong></p>
    <p><strong>硬件还能保持多久的经济用途？</strong></p>
    <p><strong>有多少折旧是真实经济折旧，而不只是会计处理？</strong></p>
    <p><strong>利用率是多少？</strong></p>
    <p><strong>如果模型或芯片迭代快于预期，这些基础设施还剩多少残值？</strong></p>
    <p>这些问题决定 AI 建设的资本效率。</p>
    <p>而当资本本身变得稀缺时，资本效率就是最重要的东西。</p>
  </div>

  <h3>{{ t('The sovereign side of the collision', '碰撞的主权一侧') }}</h3>
  <div class="pod-copy l-en">
    <p>The federal government does not have the same hurdle-rate discipline as a private company. It can continue borrowing for reasons that have nothing to do with a project's return on invested capital. That makes sovereign duration structurally different.</p>
    <p>But the market that absorbs the debt still has alternatives. A pension fund can hold Treasury bonds. It can hold investment-grade corporate debt. A Japanese insurer can own JGBs. A global investor can own cash, equities, gold or foreign sovereign debt.</p>
    <p>The U.S. government therefore retains immense financing power, but it does not get to dictate the price forever without consequence.</p>
    <p>If the required yield rises too far, the consequences begin showing up across the economy. Mortgage rates rise. Housing activity weakens. Commercial real estate refinancing gets harder. Corporate interest expense rises. High-duration equity multiples compress. Government interest expense grows. The hurdle rate for AI projects rises. The currency can begin reacting.</p>
    <p>At some point policymakers care. This is where the market enters a new stage.</p>
    <p>The question is no longer simply: <strong>What yield does the market demand?</strong></p>
    <blockquote>What do policymakers change so the market does not have to demand an even higher one?</blockquote>
    <p>They can reduce spending. They can raise taxes. They can shorten the maturity of issuance. They can create or encourage new sources of demand for government securities. They can change bank or dealer rules. Treasury can improve market liquidity. The Federal Reserve can eventually reduce short-term rates if inflation allows. In a more extreme regime, the central bank can purchase longer-duration securities.</p>
    <p>The adjustment can also appear through the currency. If investors become less willing to hold long-duration dollar claims at a given real return, the dollar itself can weaken until U.S. assets become sufficiently attractive again.</p>
    <p>That is why gold has become unusually informative. Gold has historically struggled when real yields are high because it pays no interest. If real rates rise because the market sees the United States as offering an increasingly attractive real return, gold should face a headwind. But if long yields rise partly because investors demand more compensation for fiscal or duration risk, gold can behave differently.</p>
    <p>The same nominal yield can contain two very different messages. A 5% long yield produced by strong real growth and credible monetary policy is one thing. A 5% long yield produced by a deteriorating balance between debt supply and demand is another. The market is now trying to determine which component is becoming more important.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pod-copy l-zh">
    <p>联邦政府没有私营企业那样的门槛收益率纪律。它可以继续借款，而借款理由与某个项目的投资资本回报毫无关系。因此，主权久期在结构上不同。</p>
    <p>但吸收债务的市场仍有其他选择。养老基金可以持有美债，也可以持有投资级公司债；日本保险公司可以持有日本国债；全球投资者可以持有现金、股票、黄金或其他国家的主权债。</p>
    <p>因此，美国政府仍拥有巨大的融资能力，但它不可能永远无代价地指定价格。</p>
    <p>如果市场要求的收益率升得太高，后果就开始在整个经济中显现：按揭利率上升，住房活动转弱，商业地产再融资更难，企业利息支出增加，高久期股票估值倍数收缩，政府利息支出增长，AI 项目的门槛收益率上升，汇率也可能开始反应。</p>
    <p>到某个时点，政策制定者会在意。市场由此进入新阶段。</p>
    <p>问题不再只是：<strong>市场要求什么收益率？</strong></p>
    <blockquote>政策制定者要改变什么，才能让市场不必要求一个更高的收益率？</blockquote>
    <p>他们可以削减支出、提高税收、缩短发行期限、创造或鼓励政府债券的新需求来源、改变银行或交易商规则；财政部可以改善市场流动性；若通胀允许，美联储最终可以降低短期利率；在更极端的体制下，央行可以购买更长期证券。</p>
    <p>调整也可能通过汇率发生。如果投资者越来越不愿意在既定实际回报下持有长期美元债权，美元本身就可能走弱，直到美国资产重新变得足够有吸引力。</p>
    <p>这就是黄金突然变得格外有信息量的原因。黄金不付利息，历史上往往在实际收益率高企时受压。如果实际利率上升是因为市场认为美国提供了越来越有吸引力的实际回报，黄金理应面临逆风。但如果长期收益率上升，部分原因是投资者要求更高的财政或久期风险补偿，黄金就可能表现不同。</p>
    <p>同一个名义收益率可以包含两条截然不同的信息。由强劲实际增长与可信货币政策形成的 5% 长端收益率是一回事；由债券供需平衡恶化形成的 5% 又是另一回事。市场正在判断哪一部分正在变得更重要。</p>
  </div>

  <h3>{{ t('The four-quadrant signal', '四象限信号') }}</h3>
  <div class="pod-copy">
    <p>{{ t('One of the simplest ways to watch this is to compare the direction of long yields with the direction of the dollar. If long yields rise and the dollar rises, the signal is mostly conventional tightening. The United States is paying investors more, and capital is still attracted to the currency. If long yields fall and the dollar rises, the market is often seeking safety. If long yields fall and the dollar falls, the market is pricing easier financial conditions or some form of monetary accommodation.',
      '观察这一点最简单的方法之一，是把长期收益率方向与美元方向放在一起看。长端收益率上升、美元也上升，主要是常规紧缩：美国付给投资者更多回报，资本仍被该货币吸引。长端收益率下降、美元上升，市场往往在寻求安全。长端收益率下降、美元也下降，则通常是在定价更宽松的金融条件或某种货币缓解。') }}</p>
  </div>

