    <p>{{ t('It is not a claim that the United States is about to default. It is not a claim that Treasury has imposed yield-curve control. It is not a claim that the Federal Reserve has begun monetizing the debt. It is not a claim that foreign investors have abandoned Treasuries. And it is not a claim that every rise in gold is evidence of fiscal collapse.',
      '它不是声称美国即将违约；不是声称财政部已经实施收益率曲线控制；不是声称美联储已经开始债务货币化；不是声称外国投资者已经抛弃美债；也不是声称黄金每一次上涨都是财政崩溃的证据。') }}</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pod-copy">
    <p>{{ t('Those conclusions go beyond the evidence. The current U.S. credit market remains healthy enough to make that clear. Broad high-yield spreads are still tight. The VIX remains relatively low. Equity funds continue attracting capital. Treasury auctions still function. Foreign ownership of U.S. debt remains enormous.',
      '这些结论超出了证据。当前美国信用市场仍足够健康，足以说明这一点：广义高收益债利差仍窄，VIX 相对较低，股票基金继续吸引资本，美债拍卖仍正常运行，外国持有的美国债务仍极其庞大。') }}</p>
    <p>{{ t('What we are seeing is earlier in the process. The market-clearing price of duration has risen far enough that government financing, AI financing and monetary policy are beginning to interact in ways that were mostly theoretical two months ago.',
      '我们看到的是这个过程更早的阶段。久期的市场出清价格已经上升到足够高，使政府融资、AI 融资与货币政策开始以两个月前大多还停留在理论层面的方式相互作用。') }}</p>
    <p>{{ t('That is why Ray Dalio’s recent work on sovereign debt dynamics feels more relevant today than it did at the beginning of summer. But it is also why the next step cannot simply be to declare that the endgame has arrived. The better question is: <strong>How does the system attempt to avoid the endgame?</strong>',
      '这就是为什么 Ray Dalio 最近关于主权债务动力学的研究，比初夏时更贴近当下。但也正因如此，下一步不能只是宣布终局已经到来。更好的问题是：<strong>系统会如何尝试避免终局？</strong>') }}</p>
    <p>{{ t('That is where Treasury’s reaction function, the Federal Reserve’s inflation constraint and the stablecoin architecture become central. And that is where we turn next.',
      '财政部的反应函数、美联储的通胀约束与稳定币架构，正是在这里成为核心。接下来我们转向这些机制。') }}</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="policy"><span class="num">4</span>{{ t('The policy trap', '政策困局') }}</h2>
  <div class="pod-copy l-en">
    <p>Ray Dalio's recent essay on how countries move through late debt cycles arrived at an unusually useful moment.</p>
    <p>The attraction of his framework is not that it predicts an imminent American default. The United States issues the world's dominant reserve currency, possesses an enormous tax base, deep capital markets and productive assets that make a simple emerging-market sovereign analogy misleading.</p>
    <p>The useful part is the mechanism.</p>
    <p>A government can accumulate debt for a long time without crisis so long as buyers willingly hold that debt at an acceptable yield. The critical transition comes when the quantity of debt that must be sold begins rising faster than demand at the prevailing price. Then the bond market clears through a higher yield.</p>
    <p>At first, that can look healthy. Higher yields attract new buyers. They increase expected returns for savers. They discipline speculative capital. But higher yields also increase the government's own borrowing cost.</p>
    <p>If enough debt has to be refinanced, interest expense begins compounding into the deficit that caused the supply problem in the first place.</p>
    <p>The state then faces a difficult set of choices. It can reduce spending. It can raise taxes. It can issue debt at shorter maturities where demand is stronger. It can change financial regulation to make sovereign debt easier or more attractive to hold. It can tolerate higher long yields and accept the resulting economic slowdown. Or, if inflation allows, monetary policy can eventually make financing conditions easier.</p>
    <p>The least attractive possibility is attempting to suppress nominal yields while inflation and fiscal demand remain too strong. History shows why that is dangerous: if the private market will not willingly finance a low yield, someone else has to absorb the securities or the currency has to bear more of the adjustment.</p>
    <p>Dalio's framework is particularly relevant because several of the early symptoms he emphasizes have appeared together. Long U.S. yields have moved toward multi-decade highs. The dollar has weakened from its June peak. Gold has accelerated. Japanese holdings of Treasuries declined in the most recent available data. Treasury has enlarged buyback operations in the long end.</p>
    <p>But there is an important difference between recognizing those symptoms and declaring that the late-stage outcome is already here. It is not.</p>
    <p>Foreign ownership of U.S. Treasuries remains enormous and was still higher year over year in the latest data despite a monthly decline. The broad U.S. high-yield market remains calm. The VIX remains subdued. Treasury auctions have not stopped functioning. The United States can still issue at scale.</p>
    <p>That means the useful interpretation is not: <strong>the debt crisis has begun.</strong></p>
    <blockquote>the market is beginning to test how the system will adapt before a debt crisis is allowed to begin.</blockquote>
    <p>That makes the policy response more important than the headline yield.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pod-copy l-zh">
    <p>Ray Dalio 最近关于国家如何走过债务周期后段的文章，出现得恰逢其时。</p>
    <p>这个框架的吸引力不在于预测美国即将违约。美国发行全球主导储备货币，拥有庞大税基、深厚资本市场与生产性资产，简单套用新兴市场主权危机类比会误导。</p>
    <p>真正有用的是机制。</p>
    <p>只要买家愿意按可接受收益率持有债务，政府就可以长期累积债务而不发生危机。关键转折发生在必须出售的债务数量开始快于按现价计算的需求增长之时。随后，债券市场通过更高收益率出清。</p>
    <p>起初，这甚至看起来很健康。更高收益率吸引新买家，提高储蓄者的预期回报，并约束投机资本。但更高收益率也会提高政府自身的借款成本。</p>
    <p>如果需要再融资的债务足够多，利息支出就会复利式叠加进最初造成供给问题的赤字。</p>
    <p>国家于是面对一组艰难选择：削减支出、提高税收、在需求更强的短期限发行债务、修改金融监管使主权债更容易或更值得持有、容忍更高长端收益率并接受随之而来的经济放缓；或者，在通胀允许时，由货币政策最终放松融资条件。</p>
    <p>最不理想的选择，是在通胀与财政需求仍过强时试图压低名义收益率。历史说明了危险：如果私营市场不愿按低收益率融资，就必须有人吸收这些证券，或者让汇率承担更多调整。</p>
    <p>Dalio 的框架尤其相关，是因为他强调的几个早期症状同时出现：美国长端收益率接近数十年高位，美元较 6 月高点走弱，黄金加速，日本持有美债在最新数据中下降，财政部扩大了长端回购操作。</p>
    <p>但识别这些症状，与宣布后期结果已经到来之间，存在重要差别。它尚未到来。</p>
    <p>外国持有美债的规模仍极其庞大，尽管环比下降，最新数据仍同比增长。美国广义高收益债市场保持平静，VIX 仍受抑，美债拍卖并未停止运转，美国仍能大规模发行。</p>
    <p>因此，有用的解释不是：<strong>债务危机已经开始。</strong></p>
    <blockquote>市场正开始测试：在债务危机被允许发生之前，系统会如何适应。</blockquote>
    <p>这使政策反应比标题收益率更加重要。</p>
  </div>

  <h3>{{ t('Treasury is no longer a passive character', '财政部不再是被动角色') }}</h3>
  <div class="pod-copy l-en">
    <p>For most investors, the macro story is built around the Federal Reserve. The Fed raises rates. The Fed cuts rates. The Fed shrinks its balance sheet. The Fed expands it. Treasury is often treated as the ministry that simply supplies the bonds the government requires.</p>
    <p>That separation is becoming less useful. Treasury cannot set the long-term yield in an open market. But it can influence the composition and functioning of the market that produces that yield.</p>
    <p>It chooses how much of the government's borrowing is financed through bills versus notes and bonds. It can conduct buybacks that remove older, less-liquid securities. It can change auction patterns. It can respond to investor demand across maturities. It can support market liquidity. And it can work with the regulatory system that determines how easily banks and dealers intermediate Treasury securities.</p>
    <p>That does not give Treasury the power to repeal the bond market. It does mean Treasury has tools that matter before the Federal Reserve needs to intervene.</p>
    <p>The expanded long-duration buyback program is the clearest recent example. Treasury has said that, beginning in September, it will at least double the maximum size of certain liquidity-support buybacks in the 10-to-30-year part of the curve.</p>
    <p>The announcement came after the 30-year yield pushed toward roughly 5.34%. The market initially responded by buying long bonds. Then much of the move reversed.</p>
    <p>That reversal is more interesting than a lasting rally would have been. If a relatively modest buyback program could permanently force the 30-year yield lower, the underlying problem would likely be a simple liquidity or dealer-capacity issue. Instead, the market quickly returned to asking the same questions about inflation, deficits, issuance and demand.</p>
    <p>Treasury therefore revealed two things at once. First, it is willing to become more active when long-end market functioning becomes uncomfortable. Second, the current pressure is larger than a small liquidity operation can solve.</p>
    <p>That is why we refer to an emerging <strong>Treasury reaction function</strong>.</p>
    <p>There is no official 5.3% cap. No policymaker has promised to defend a particular yield. And the buybacks should not be misrepresented as quantitative easing.</p>
    <p>Treasury has to finance itself elsewhere. The operation can change maturity composition and improve liquidity; it does not create central-bank reserves out of nothing.</p>
    <p>But the market now has more information than it had before the announcement. It knows that sufficiently uncomfortable long-end conditions can change Treasury behavior. Investors will begin trading that behavior just as they trade the Fed's.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pod-copy l-zh">
    <p>对大多数投资者而言，宏观故事一直围绕美联储构建：美联储加息、降息、缩表、扩表。财政部常被当成只是供应政府所需债券的部门。</p>
    <p>这种切割越来越没有用。财政部不能在开放市场中设定长期收益率，却可以影响产生该收益率的市场结构与功能。</p>
    <p>它决定多少政府借款用国库券融资、多少用中长期票据与债券融资；可以回购较旧、流动性较差的证券；可以改变拍卖模式；可以回应不同期限的投资者需求；可以支持市场流动性；也可以与监管体系协作，改变银行与交易商中介美债的难易程度。</p>
    <p>这并不赋予财政部废除债券市场的权力，却意味着在美联储需要介入前，财政部已经有重要工具。</p>
    <p>扩大长期债券回购计划，是最近最清楚的例子。财政部表示，从 9 月起，将至少把收益率曲线 10 至 30 年区间某些流动性支持回购的最高规模翻倍。</p>
    <p>公告发布前，30 年期收益率曾逼近约 5.34%。市场最初买入长债，随后大部分涨幅回吐。</p>
    <p>这次回吐比持续上涨更有信息量。如果一个相对温和的回购计划能永久压低 30 年期收益率，底层问题很可能只是流动性或交易商容量。事实却是，市场很快重新追问通胀、赤字、发行与需求。</p>
    <p>因此，财政部同时透露了两点：第一，长端市场功能变得令人不适时，它愿意更积极行动；第二，当前压力大于一次小规模流动性操作能够解决的程度。</p>
    <p>这就是我们把它称为新兴<strong>财政部反应函数</strong>的原因。</p>
    <p>不存在官方 5.3% 上限。没有政策制定者承诺捍卫某个收益率。回购也不应被误称为量化宽松。</p>
    <p>财政部必须在别处为自己融资。操作可以改变期限结构、改善流动性，却不会凭空创造央行准备金。</p>
    <p>但市场现在掌握了公告前没有的信息：足够令人不适的长端条件，会改变财政部行为。投资者会像交易美联储反应一样交易这种行为。</p>
  </div>

