      <path class="pod-arrow pod-arrow-gold pod-draw" d="M627 345 C627 403 516 414 447 414" marker-end="url(#f4b)"/>
      <rect class="pod-node" x="326" y="385" width="168" height="61" rx="14"/>
      <text class="pod-svg-text" x="410" y="412" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="800"><tspan class="l-en">FINANCIAL</tspan><tspan class="l-zh">金融条件</tspan></text>
      <text class="pod-svg-text" x="410" y="432" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="800"><tspan class="l-en">CONDITIONS</tspan><tspan class="l-zh">共同出清</tspan></text>
      <text class="pod-svg-text" x="410" y="481" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="800" fill="var(--pod-red)"><tspan class="l-en">LIQUIDITY SUPPORT ≠ QE ≠ FORMAL YIELD-CURVE CONTROL</tspan><tspan class="l-zh">流动性支持 ≠ QE ≠ 正式收益率曲线控制</tspan></text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption>{{ t('The institutions can react to the same long-yield move differently because their mandates differ. Treasury buybacks can improve liquidity and change maturity composition; they do not create reserves, are not QE, and do not establish an official yield cap. [F/I]',
      '两家机构的使命不同，因此可以对同一轮长端收益率变动作出不同反应。财政部回购能够改善流动性、改变期限结构；它不会创造准备金，不是 QE，也没有建立官方收益率上限。[F/I]') }}</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="pod-copy l-en">
    <p>This is where the macro problem becomes genuinely unusual. The Federal Reserve and Treasury do not optimize the same variable. The Fed cares about inflation, employment and the stability of expectations. Treasury cares about financing the government and preserving a liquid, functional government-securities market. Most of the time these goals coexist comfortably. Not always.</p>
    <p>Imagine long Treasury yields rise because inflation expectations are rising. The Federal Reserve may consider that market move useful. Higher long rates tighten financial conditions. Mortgages get more expensive. Risky investment slows. Demand cools. The bond market is helping the Fed.</p>
    <p>Treasury can experience the same move differently. Higher long rates raise the government's future interest burden. They can worsen housing affordability. They can reduce demand at long-maturity auctions. They can impair liquidity in older bonds. And they can create political pressure because the cost of financing the economy is rising.</p>
    <p>That creates a potential tension: <strong>The Fed may want financial conditions to remain tight enough to restrain inflation. Treasury may want the long end to remain orderly and affordable enough to finance the government and the economy.</strong></p>
    <p>Neither institution needs to be acting improperly for this tension to exist. Their mandates are simply different.</p>
    <p>The next several months will show whether those objectives can be reconciled without a larger policy shift.</p>
    <p>If inflation continues cooling, the problem becomes much easier. The Fed can eventually lower the short rate. Real yields can fall. The long end can stabilize without the market interpreting policy as inflationary. That is the benign path.</p>
    <p>If inflation stays too high, the policy trade becomes harder. Treasury can improve the plumbing. It can shift issuance. It can create more demand at the short end. But it cannot make inflation disappear.</p>
    <p>And if investors believe attempts to lower long borrowing costs will weaken inflation discipline, long yields can perversely rise even as policymakers try to push financial conditions lower. This is the classic problem of trying to control the nominal price of capital without controlling the forces creating inflation.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pod-copy l-zh">
    <p>宏观问题在这里真正变得不同寻常。美联储与财政部优化的不是同一个变量。美联储关注通胀、就业与预期稳定；财政部关注政府融资，以及维持一个流动、功能正常的政府证券市场。多数时候，两组目标可以舒适共存，但并非永远如此。</p>
    <p>假设长端美债收益率因通胀预期上升而上升。美联储可能认为这种市场变动有用：更高长端利率收紧金融条件，按揭更贵，风险投资放缓，需求降温，债券市场在帮美联储。</p>
    <p>财政部对同一变动的感受不同。更高长端利率提高政府未来利息负担，恶化住房可负担性，降低长期限拍卖需求，损害旧券流动性，并因经济融资成本上升而制造政治压力。</p>
    <p>于是潜在张力出现：<strong>美联储可能希望金融条件保持足够紧，以抑制通胀；财政部可能希望长端足够有序、可负担，以便为政府与经济融资。</strong></p>
    <p>这种张力不要求任何一家机构行为不当。它们的使命只是不同。</p>
    <p>未来几个月将展示，这些目标能否在不发生更大政策转向的情况下得到协调。</p>
    <p>如果通胀继续降温，问题会容易得多。美联储最终可以降低短端利率，实际收益率可以下降，长端可以稳定，而市场不必把政策解读为通胀性的。这是温和路径。</p>
    <p>如果通胀居高不下，政策取舍会更困难。财政部可以改善管道、调整发行、在短端创造更多需求，却不能让通胀消失。</p>
    <p>如果投资者认为降低长期借款成本的尝试会削弱通胀纪律，那么即便政策制定者试图放松金融条件，长期收益率也可能反常上升。这正是试图控制资本名义价格、却没有控制通胀驱动力的经典难题。</p>
  </div>

  <h3>{{ t('Why 1998 is only half the analogy', '为什么 1998 只是一半的类比') }}</h3>
  <div class="pod-copy">
    <p>{{ t('Our earlier reports leaned on 1998 because the structure was attractive. A technology investment boom was alive. A financial-market shock created tightening. The Federal Reserve eased into that shock. The technology cycle then extended into a much larger final boom. That remains a useful upside analogy.',
      '早期报告偏重 1998 年，因为结构很有吸引力：科技投资热潮仍在，金融市场冲击制造紧缩，美联储对冲击进行宽松，科技周期随后延伸成更大的最终繁荣。这仍是有用的上行情景类比。') }}</p>
    <p>{{ t('But it leaves out the critical difference. Inflation in 1998 was sufficiently contained that the Fed had room to ease without immediately threatening its credibility. Today, the Fed does not have that freedom. Energy remains a problem. Core inflation remains above target. The labor market is weakening, but not in an environment of clean price stability. That means the 1998 playbook cannot simply be repeated on command.',
      '但它遗漏了关键差别。1998 年通胀受到充分控制，美联储可以宽松而不立刻威胁自身信誉。今天，美联储没有这份自由。能源仍是问题，核心通胀仍高于目标，就业市场正在转弱，却并不处于干净的价格稳定环境。因此，1998 年剧本不能被按按钮重演。') }}</p>
  </div>
  <h3>{{ t('Why the 1940s are only half the analogy too', '为什么 1940 年代也只是一半的类比') }}</h3>
  <div class="pod-copy">
    <p>{{ t('The other tempting analogy is the era when the Federal Reserve effectively capped Treasury yields to help finance World War II. That history demonstrates the power—and cost—of explicit yield control. If a central bank commits to a cap that private investors do not accept, the central bank has to purchase whatever quantity of bonds is necessary to enforce it. The constraint moves from the interest rate to the balance sheet and, ultimately, to inflation or the currency.',
      '另一个诱人类比，是美联储为资助二战而实际限制美债收益率的年代。那段历史展示了显式收益率控制的力量与代价。如果央行承诺一个私营投资者不接受的上限，就必须购买执行上限所需的任意数量债券。约束会从利率转移到资产负债表，最终转移到通胀或汇率。') }}</p>
    <p>{{ t('But the present United States is nowhere near that regime. There is no formal long-bond peg. The Fed is not promising unlimited purchases. Treasury buybacks are not central-bank monetization. Using the 1940s as a description of today’s market would be dramatic and wrong.',
      '但当前美国远未进入该体制。不存在正式长债锚定，美联储没有承诺无限购买，财政部回购也不是央行货币化。用 1940 年代描述今天的市场，既戏剧化又错误。') }}</p>
    <p>{{ t('Using them as a boundary condition is useful. They tell us what becomes possible <strong>if</strong> ordinary market absorption fails and policymakers later decide that high nominal yields themselves are unacceptable. We are not there. The point of this report is to watch the path before we get there.',
      '把它当作边界条件则有用。它告诉我们：<strong>如果</strong>正常市场吸收失败、政策制定者后来认定高名义收益率本身不可接受，什么会变得可能。我们还没到那里。本报告的目的，是观察抵达那里之前的路径。') }}</p>
  </div>

  <h3>{{ t('Inflation is the veto', '通胀拥有否决权') }}</h3>
  <div class="pod-copy l-en">
    <p>The entire system turns on inflation because inflation determines how much freedom policymakers have.</p>
    <p>If inflation falls convincingly, the number of available solutions expands. The Fed can lower short rates. Real yields can fall without the dollar necessarily collapsing. Treasury can finance more easily. Corporate credit becomes cheaper. AI project hurdle rates fall. Gold can continue appreciating through lower real yields without necessarily signaling fiscal panic. Bitcoin can benefit from improving liquidity. The economy can move toward a conventional easing cycle.</p>
    <p>If inflation does not fall, every option becomes more dangerous. The labor market can weaken and still fail to produce rate cuts. Treasury can attempt to improve long-end liquidity and still see the currency weaken. Long rates can remain high because investors demand compensation for inflation. Gold can initially correct if the Fed responds by pushing real rates even higher. Risk assets can struggle even though nominal GDP remains strong.</p>
    <p>This is why the recent energy shock matters far beyond oil. Oil is only the first input. Refining margins matter. Diesel matters. Gasoline matters. Transport costs matter. Utilities matter. Food distribution matters. The more those costs pass through into the broader price level, the harder it becomes for the Fed to respond to labor weakness.</p>
    <p>The danger is a policy environment in which the economy is weak enough to want relief but inflation is high enough to deny it.</p>
    <p>That is the <strong>inflation veto</strong>. It is one of the four regimes we will return to later in the report.</p>
    <p>For now, the important point is simpler. Treasury may be developing more tools to manage the long end. But Treasury cannot overrule the inflation constraint.</p>
    <p>That is why stablecoins are so interesting. They offer a way to change <strong>who buys the debt and which maturity they buy</strong> without requiring the Federal Reserve to immediately make money easier.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pod-copy l-zh">
    <p>整个系统围绕通胀转动，因为通胀决定政策制定者拥有多大自由。</p>
    <p>如果通胀可信地下降，可用方案会增加。美联储可以降低短端利率；实际收益率可以下降而美元未必崩溃；财政部融资更容易；企业信贷变便宜；AI 项目门槛收益率下降；黄金可以在实际收益率下降时继续升值，而不必然意味着财政恐慌；比特币可以受益于流动性改善；经济可以走向常规宽松周期。</p>
    <p>如果通胀不降，每个选项都更危险。就业市场可能转弱却仍换不来降息；财政部可能改善长端流动性却仍看到美元走弱；长期利率可能因投资者要求通胀补偿而保持高位；如果美联储以更高实际利率回应，黄金起初可能大幅回调；即便名义 GDP 强劲，风险资产也可能挣扎。</p>
    <p>这就是近期能源冲击意义远超原油的原因。原油只是第一个投入：炼油利润、柴油、汽油、运输成本、公用事业、食品流通都重要。这些成本越多传导进整体价格水平，美联储就越难回应就业转弱。</p>
    <p>危险在于一种政策环境：经济弱到需要缓解，通胀却高到拒绝缓解。</p>
    <p>这就是<strong>通胀否决权</strong>。它是本报告稍后回到的四种体制之一。</p>
    <p>此刻更简单的重点是：财政部也许正在发展更多管理长端的工具，但财政部不能推翻通胀约束。</p>
    <p>这正是稳定币如此有趣的原因。它们提供了一种改变<strong>谁买债、买哪个期限</strong>的办法，而不要求美联储立刻把资金变得更宽松。</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="stablecoins"><span class="num">5</span>{{ t('Stablecoins and the Treasury bill machine', '稳定币与国库券机器') }}</h2>
  <div class="pod-copy l-en">
    <p>Stablecoins are usually discussed as part of the cryptocurrency market. That description is becoming incomplete.</p>
    <p>Their more important macroeconomic role may be as a new distribution system for dollar liabilities backed by short-term U.S. government assets.</p>
    <p>That sounds technical. The mechanism is straightforward.</p>
    <p>A regulated dollar stablecoin promises the holder something economically close to a digital dollar. To make that promise credible, the issuer has to hold safe, liquid reserves. Under the emerging U.S. framework, those reserves are concentrated in cash-like instruments: bank deposits, repos and short-dated Treasury securities.</p>
    <p>That means every new dollar of stablecoin supply can create demand for the assets held behind it. If stablecoins grow from hundreds of billions of dollars into the multi-trillion-dollar system policymakers envision, the reserve pool becomes macroeconomically significant.</p>
    <p>This matters to Treasury. But not in the simplistic way sometimes advertised.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pod-copy l-zh">
    <p>稳定币通常被当作加密货币市场的一部分讨论。这种描述正在变得不完整。</p>
    <p>它们更重要的宏观角色，可能是成为一套新的美元负债分销系统，而这些负债由短期美国政府资产支持。</p>
    <p>听起来技术性很强，机制其实直接。</p>
    <p>受监管的美元稳定币向持有人承诺一种经济上接近数字美元的资产。要让承诺可信，发行方必须持有安全且流动的储备。在正在形成的美国框架下，这些储备集中于类现金工具：银行存款、回购协议与短期美债。</p>
    <p>因此，每新增一美元稳定币供给，都可能为其背后的资产创造需求。如果稳定币从数千亿美元增长为政策制定者设想的数万亿美元系统，储备池将在宏观层面变得重要。</p>
    <p>这对财政部重要，但不是某些宣传所描述的简单方式。</p>
  </div>

  <div class="pod-callout" style="--pod-c:var(--pod-red)">
    <b>{{ t('Maturity law', '期限法则') }}</b>
    <p>{{ t('Stablecoin bill demand is not equivalent to 30-year Treasury demand. A redeemable digital dollar naturally holds bills, repo and cash—not large unhedged positions in volatile long bonds.',
      '稳定币对国库券的需求，不等于对 30 年期美债的需求。可随时赎回的数字美元天然持有国库券、回购与现金，而不是大量未对冲、价格波动明显的长期债券。') }}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="pod-copy l-en">
    <h3>Stablecoins do not naturally buy 30-year bonds</h3>
    <p>A stablecoin can be redeemed quickly. A 30-year Treasury can fluctuate substantially in price. Backing on-demand digital dollars with large amounts of long-duration bonds would create an obvious asset-liability mismatch.</p>
    <p>So stablecoin issuers naturally prefer bills, repos and other extremely short-duration government collateral. That means stablecoins do <strong>not</strong> directly solve the problem of finding enough investors willing to hold 20- or 30-year Treasury bonds.</p>
    <p>They can help Treasury in a different way.</p>
    <p>Suppose the short end develops a much larger structural buyer base. Treasury has greater flexibility to finance part of the government's borrowing through bills instead of forcing every incremental financing need into longer notes and bonds. More bill demand can therefore reduce the amount of marginal duration the market has to absorb.</p>
    <p>Not eliminate it. Reduce it.</p>
    <p>That distinction is the key to understanding why stablecoins matter to the current report.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pod-copy l-zh">
    <h3>稳定币并不天然购买 30 年期债券</h3>
    <p>稳定币可以快速赎回，30 年期美债价格却可能大幅波动。用大量长久期债券支持按需赎回的数字美元，会形成明显的资产负债期限错配。</p>
    <p>因此，稳定币发行方天然偏好国库券、回购与其他极短久期政府抵押品。这意味着稳定币<strong>不能</strong>直接解决寻找足够投资者持有 20 或 30 年期美债的问题。</p>
    <p>它们可以用另一种方式帮助财政部。</p>
    <p>假设短端形成了一个大得多的结构性买家群体。财政部便更有弹性用国库券为部分政府借款融资，而不必把每一笔增量融资需求都推向更长期票据与债券。因此，更多国库券需求可以减少市场边际上必须吸收的久期数量。</p>
    <p>不是消除，而是减少。</p>
    <p>这个区别，是理解稳定币为何与本报告相关的关键。</p>
  </div>

