  <div class="pod-copy">
    <p>{{ t('Memory should therefore be analyzed through four different clocks. The first is <strong>physical supply and pricing</strong>: are HBM, DRAM and NAND actually becoming abundant? If supply remains tight, supplier economics can remain powerful. The second is <strong>supplier free cash flow</strong>: how much of the pricing power is turning into real cash after capital expenditure? This clock can remain strong even after the stock peaks.',
      '因此，存储应通过四只不同时钟分析。第一只是<strong>物理供给与定价</strong>：HBM、DRAM 与 NAND 是否真的正在变得充足？如果供给仍紧，供应商经济性可以保持强劲。第二只是<strong>供应商自由现金流</strong>：定价权在资本开支后有多少转化为真实现金？这只钟可以在股票见顶后继续强劲。') }}</p>
    <p>{{ t('The third is <strong>equity positioning and valuation</strong>: how much of the good news has already been priced? This clock can turn first. The fourth is <strong>downstream customer cost</strong>: if memory suppliers keep earning extraordinary margins, someone else is paying them. That raises the cost of AI servers and data-center systems. In other words, a good memory cycle can become a headwind for the customer’s return on AI investment.',
      '第三只是<strong>股票仓位与估值</strong>：好消息有多少已经定价？这只钟可以最先转向。第四只是<strong>下游客户成本</strong>：如果存储供应商持续赚取异常高利润，就必然有人在付钱；这会提高 AI 服务器与数据中心系统成本。换句话说，强劲存储周期可以变成客户 AI 投资回报的逆风。') }}</p>
    <p>{{ t('Those clocks are connected. They are not synchronized. That is the mistake our earlier framework made.',
      '这些时钟彼此连接，却不同步。这正是旧框架犯的错误。') }}</p>
    <p>{{ t('The next good Korean memory entry may appear while the fundamental news still looks strong. That sounds counterintuitive. If the equity positioning has already reset while contract pricing, HBM demand and supplier cash flow remain strong, the investor suddenly owns a good business at a much less dangerous price.',
      '下一次好的韩国存储入场点，可能在基本面新闻仍然强劲时出现。这听起来违反直觉。如果股票仓位已经重置，而合约定价、HBM 需求与供应商现金流保持强劲，投资者会突然以危险性低得多的价格持有好企业。') }}</p>
    <p>{{ t('The confirmation we would want is not simply another record earnings report. We would want to see foreign selling stabilize; retail leverage normalize; share-price volatility fall; memory contract economics remain firm; and management capital returns remain disciplined. That would indicate that the equity cycle has reset without the profit cycle collapsing. That is a much better setup than buying at the top simply because the earnings are good.',
      '我们要的确认不只是又一份创纪录财报，而是外资卖出稳定、散户杠杆正常化、股价波动下降、存储合约经济性保持稳健、管理层资本回报保持纪律。这将表明股票周期已经重置而利润周期未坍塌，比仅因盈利漂亮就在顶部买入好得多。') }}</p>
  </div>

  <h3>{{ t('Japan: the global funding variable returns', '日本：全球融资变量回归') }}</h3>
  <div class="pod-copy l-en">
    <p>Japan has spent most of the last generation behaving like an exporter of cheap capital. Domestic yields were so low that Japanese institutions had powerful incentives to look abroad. That made Japan an important buyer of foreign bonds and an important funding source for global carry trades.</p>
    <p>The end of zero rates changes that structure. When Japanese government bonds offer meaningful yields again, domestic assets become more competitive. A Japanese institution that once had almost no reason to hold long-term domestic government debt now has a real alternative.</p>
    <p>That matters to the U.S. Treasury market. The investor does not need to become bearish on the United States. The investor only needs to decide that the domestic risk-adjusted return has improved enough to justify holding more capital at home. That reduces one source of marginal demand for foreign duration.</p>
    <h3>The yen matters because it funds leverage</h3>
    <p>The second channel is the currency. For years, investors could borrow yen cheaply and use the proceeds to buy higher-yielding assets elsewhere.</p>
    <p>That works beautifully while Japanese rates are low; the yen is stable or weakening; and global risk assets rise. It becomes dangerous when the funding currency strengthens.</p>
    <p>If the BOJ remains comparatively hawkish while U.S. policy moves toward lower real rates, the rate differential narrows. The yen can strengthen. Carry positions become less attractive. Investors unwind leverage.</p>
    <p>That can produce a strange market combination: the dollar weakens; gold rises; U.S. rate expectations improve; and global equities still sell off temporarily because the funding structure is deleveraging.</p>
    <p>That is not contradictory. It is a reminder that liquidity conditions depend on more than the Fed.</p>
    <h3>Japan has its own duration problem too</h3>
    <p>The irony is that Japan is not merely a source of capital for the U.S. duration problem. It has a sovereign-duration problem of its own.</p>
    <p>Higher JGB yields increase Japanese government financing costs. They change bank balance sheets. They change insurer portfolios. They change pension allocations. They change the currency.</p>
    <p>That creates a two-way feedback loop. Higher Japanese yields can pull capital home. That can reduce demand for Treasuries. Higher Treasury yields can then tighten global conditions. A global risk shock can strengthen the yen and alter the BOJ's path.</p>
    <p>The two largest developed sovereign bond markets increasingly interact through capital flows rather than existing in separate macro stories. That is why Japan deserves a central place in the current U.S. market framework.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pod-copy l-zh">
    <p>过去一代的大部分时间里，日本像廉价资本出口国。国内收益率太低，日本机构有强烈动力向海外寻找机会。因此，日本成为外国债券的重要买家，也是全球套息交易的重要融资来源。</p>
    <p>零利率结束改变了这个结构。当日本国债再次提供有意义的收益率，国内资产竞争力提高。曾经几乎没有理由持有长期本国政府债的日本机构，现在拥有真实替代方案。</p>
    <p>这关系到美国国债市场。投资者无需看空美国，只需认为国内风险调整后回报改善到足以让更多资本留在本土，就会减少外国久期的一处边际需求来源。</p>
    <h3>日元重要，因为它为杠杆融资</h3>
    <p>第二条渠道是汇率。多年来，投资者可以低成本借入日元，再用资金买入其他地方更高收益的资产。</p>
    <p>当日本利率低、日元稳定或走弱、全球风险资产上涨时，这很好用；融资货币走强时，它就变得危险。</p>
    <p>如果日本央行相对鹰派，而美国政策走向更低实际利率，利差收窄，日元可以走强，套息仓位吸引力下降，投资者解除杠杆。</p>
    <p>这会产生看似奇怪的组合：美元走弱、黄金上涨、美国利率预期改善，全球股票却因融资结构去杠杆而暂时下跌。</p>
    <p>这并不矛盾，而是提醒我们：流动性条件不只取决于美联储。</p>
    <h3>日本也有自己的久期问题</h3>
    <p>讽刺的是，日本不只是美国久期问题的资本来源，它也有自己的主权久期问题。</p>
    <p>更高日本国债收益率提高政府融资成本，改变银行资产负债表、保险组合、养老金配置与汇率。</p>
    <p>这形成双向反馈：更高日本收益率把资本拉回国内，可能减少美债需求；更高美债收益率随后收紧全球条件；全球风险冲击又可能强化日元、改变日本央行路径。</p>
    <p>两个最大的发达经济体主权债市场，越来越多地通过资本流相互作用，而不是活在独立宏观故事中。这正是日本必须处于当前美国市场框架中心的原因。</p>
  </div>

