  <div class="pod-copy l-en">
    <h3>Regime A — Managed Duration Pressure <sup class="ev ev-s">S</sup></h3>
    <p><strong>Current desk probability: 45%</strong></p>
    <p>This is our base case. The defining feature is not that long yields collapse. It is that policymakers and market structure gradually prevent long-duration stress from becoming disorderly while the Federal Reserve remains constrained by inflation.</p>
    <p>Treasury continues to manage liquidity and maturity composition. Short-duration demand deepens through money-market funds, banks, stablecoins and other pools. The long end remains expensive enough to discipline weak projects but not expensive enough to break credit. Real yields eventually stop rising. The dollar fails to recover its earlier strength. Gold remains structurally bid. Bitcoin retains a better liquidity backdrop than it had in June, but remains volatile. U.S. equities rotate and correct rather than immediately enter a systemic bear market. AI investment continues, but the market rewards companies that can show return on capital rather than merely ambition.</p>
    <p>This regime is "managed" because the system adapts. It does not require an explicit yield cap. It does not require QE. It does not even require the Fed to cut immediately. It requires the combination of Treasury financing choices, investor demand, regulatory plumbing and eventually less restrictive real rates to keep the long-end problem from turning into a credit problem.</p>
    <p>The hidden cost is that every solution changes the system. If Treasury relies more heavily on short-term financing because bill demand is strong, the government's refinancing exposure to the policy rate rises. If stablecoins become large structural buyers of bills, the dollar system gains a new financing channel but becomes more tightly linked to the health of short-term sovereign collateral. If the Fed later lowers short rates, the relief can arrive into an economy where the AI infrastructure base is already much larger than it was during the original boom.</p>
    <p>That is why this regime can be bullish without being safe. It can preserve the cycle long enough for the final stage to become larger.</p>
    <h3>Regime B — Benign Absorption <sup class="ev ev-s">S</sup></h3>
    <p><strong>Current desk probability: 20%</strong></p>
    <p>This is the cleanest outcome. The market solves the problem naturally. Inflation cools. Energy pressure fades. Labor-market weakness reduces the need for further tightening. Long Treasuries become sufficiently attractive at current yields that private and foreign demand returns. Corporate AI issuance continues but does not require increasingly large concessions. Treasury does not need to keep enlarging its market-support operations. The 30-year yield falls because investors want the bonds, not because the government has to create a stronger mechanism to support them.</p>
    <p>This is the closest path to the old 1998-style extension. Financial conditions ease. Real yields fall. The dollar stabilizes or softens gradually. Breadth improves. Small caps participate. Semiconductors can regain leadership because the discount-rate pressure diminishes while demand remains intact. Gold can continue to perform, but the urgency of the fiscal-confidence component should fade. Bitcoin increasingly behaves like a conventional high-beta liquidity asset.</p>
    <p>This is the regime that would most clearly prove that the duration-scarcity framework had been overinterpreting a temporary dislocation. It deserves a real probability precisely because the system is not yet broken.</p>
    <h3>Regime C — Inflation Veto <sup class="ev ev-s">S</sup></h3>
    <p><strong>Current desk probability: 25%</strong></p>
    <p>This is the regime we underweighted in July. The bond problem does not go away because the inflation problem refuses to cooperate. Energy remains expensive. Refined products stay tight. Core inflation fails to decelerate convincingly. The labor market weakens enough to create political pressure for easier policy but not enough to erase inflation. The Fed remains restrictive. Real yields rise again. The dollar strengthens. Long-duration equities de-rate further. Gold gives back part of its recent move. Bitcoin suffers a larger correction because it combines monetary optionality with high-beta liquidity exposure. The market begins to reward current cash flow and nominal beneficiaries while punishing distant optionality.</p>
    <p>This regime can still be consistent with the long-run gold thesis. If high real rates ultimately prove fiscally or economically unsustainable, the eventual adjustment may be larger. But the path first runs through a successful tightening of financial conditions.</p>
    <p>That is why "inflation is bullish for gold" is an incomplete rule. Inflation is bullish for gold when the policy response is inadequate or when the market loses confidence in the real value of nominal claims. Inflation can be bearish for gold when the central bank credibly raises real returns. The distinction matters.</p>
    <h3>Regime D — Term-Premium Accident <sup class="ev ev-s">S</sup></h3>
    <p><strong>Current desk probability: 10%</strong></p>
    <p>This is the real crisis branch. It is not "the 30-year yield is 5.3%." It is what happens when long-duration stress stops being contained to valuation and enters credit.</p>
    <p>The sequence matters. First, long Treasury yields remain high or rise further despite support. Then auction concessions increase and market depth worsens. Corporate spreads widen. AI-linked debt becomes more expensive. The weakest credits deteriorate. Equity breadth weakens persistently. Volatility stops mean-reverting. Then the market begins selling assets that were not part of the original problem. That is when liquidity itself becomes the scarce asset.</p>
    <p>In that environment, Bitcoin likely falls hard. Silver falls harder than gold. Korean and other high-beta equities sell violently. Even good AI infrastructure companies can be liquidated because the market is no longer distinguishing between good and bad projects. Gold may initially fall too as investors raise cash. Then the policy response becomes larger. Only after the liquidity response becomes credible should gold, Bitcoin and quality risk assets begin separating again.</p>
    <p>This is why a crisis can be structurally bullish for monetary assets and tactically devastating to them. The timing of the policy response matters as much as the direction of the long-run thesis.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pod-copy l-zh">
    <h3>体制 A — 受管理的久期压力 <sup class="ev ev-s">S</sup></h3>
    <p><strong>当前研究台概率：45%</strong></p>
    <p>这是基准情景。它的定义特征不是长端收益率崩塌，而是在美联储仍受通胀约束时，政策与市场结构逐步防止长久期压力失序。财政部继续管理流动性与到期结构；货币市场基金、银行、稳定币等加深短久期需求；长端足够贵，能约束弱项目，却还没贵到击碎信用。实际收益率最终停止上升，美元不再恢复先前强势，黄金保持结构性买盘，比特币流动性背景好于 6 月但仍波动，美股轮动与调整而非立即进入系统性熊市；AI 投资继续，但市场奖励能证明资本回报而不只是展示野心的公司。</p>
    <p>它之所以“受管理”，是因为系统在适应。它不需要明示收益率上限，不需要 QE，甚至不需要美联储立即降息；它需要财政部融资选择、投资者需求、监管管道与最终较少限制的实际利率联手，防止长端问题转成信用问题。隐性成本是每个解决方案都会改变系统：更依赖短期融资会提高政府对政策利率的再融资暴露；稳定币成为大型国库券买家会创造新渠道，也使美元体系更依赖短期主权抵押品的健康；美联储日后降低短端利率时，AI 基础设施已比最初热潮庞大得多。因此这一体制可以利多却不安全：它可以让周期延续到最后阶段变得更大。</p>
    <h3>体制 B — 良性吸收 <sup class="ev ev-s">S</sup></h3>
    <p><strong>当前研究台概率：20%</strong></p>
    <p>这是最干净的结果：市场自然解决问题。通胀降温，能源压力消退，劳动市场走弱减少进一步紧缩的必要，长期美债在当前收益率下足够吸引私人与海外需求回归。AI 企业发债继续，却无需越来越大的让步；财政部不必继续扩大市场支持操作；30 年期收益率下降，是因为投资者想买债，而非政府必须创造更强支持机制。</p>
    <p>这最接近旧的 1998 式延长：金融条件放松，实际收益率下降，美元稳定或渐软，广度改善，小盘参与，半导体在需求完好、折现率压力减弱时重获领导；黄金仍可以走强，但财政信心部分的紧迫性消退；比特币更像传统高贝塔流动性资产。这一体制最能证明久期稀缺框架过度解读了一次暂时错位。正因为系统尚未损坏，它应得一个真实概率。</p>
    <h3>体制 C — 通胀否决 <sup class="ev ev-s">S</sup></h3>
    <p><strong>当前研究台概率：25%</strong></p>
    <p>这是我们 7 月低估的体制。通胀不配合，债券问题就不会消失。能源仍贵，成品油紧张，核心通胀不能有说服力地减速；就业弱到引发政治宽松压力，却不足以抹去通胀。美联储维持限制，实际收益率重升，美元走强，长久期股票进一步降估值，黄金回吐部分涨幅，比特币因货币期权性叠加高贝塔流动性暴露而更深回调。市场奖励当期现金流与名义受益者，惩罚遥远期权。</p>
    <p>这仍可与黄金长期论点并存：若高实际利率最终在财政或经济上不可持续，最终调整可能更大，但路径会先经过金融条件的成功紧缩。所以“通胀利多黄金”只是不完整规则：政策回应不足，或市场不再信任名义债权的实际价值时，通胀利多黄金；央行可信地抬高实际回报时，通胀可以利空黄金。区别很重要。</p>
    <h3>体制 D — 期限溢价事故 <sup class="ev ev-s">S</sup></h3>
    <p><strong>当前研究台概率：10%</strong></p>
    <p>这才是真正危机分支。它不是“30 年期收益率到 5.3%”，而是长久期压力不再停留于估值，开始进入信用。顺序很重要：长期美债收益率在支持下仍高企或继续上升；拍卖让步扩大、市场深度恶化；企业利差走阔；AI 相关债务更贵；最弱信用恶化；股票广度持续走弱；波动率不再均值回归；随后市场开始抛售本不是问题源头的资产。此时稀缺品变成流动性本身。</p>
    <p>在这种环境中，比特币很可能大跌，白银跌得比黄金更猛，韩国及其他高贝塔股票剧烈抛售；即使优质 AI 基础设施公司也会被清算，因为市场已不再区分好坏项目。黄金也可能因投资者筹措现金而先跌，然后政策回应变大；只有流动性回应变得可信后，黄金、比特币与优质风险资产才应重新分化。所以危机可以在结构上利多货币资产，战术上却毁灭性打击它们。政策回应的时点，与长期论点的方向同样重要。</p>
  </div>

